/**
\fn AnimatedAttributesBase & pdg::AnimatedAttributesBase::changeFillGradient(const Point & start, const Color & startColor, const Point & end, const Color & endColor, double seconds, EasingFunc easing)
\brief Animate a linear fill gradient.

Switches the fill mode when the pending delay expires, then interpolates the numeric/color fields. Replaces other solid/gradient fill-color tracks while preserving fill opacity. Resolves lineStyle_Auto as a fill.

\param start Target gradient start point.
\param startColor Target color at the start.
\param end Target gradient end point.
\param endColor Target color at the end.
\param seconds Required finite, nonnegative duration in seconds. Zero applies the target immediately. After wait() or andThen(), duration must be greater than zero.
\param easing EasingFunc callback, such as linearTween or easeInOutQuad. Its elapsed time and duration arguments are seconds.

\return This object, for chaining.

\see changeFillRadialGradient(), changeFillColor()
*/

/**
\fn AnimatedAttributesBase & pdg::AnimatedAttributesBase::changeFillRadialGradient(const Point & center, const Color & centerColor, float radius, const Color & endColor, double seconds, EasingFunc easing)
\brief Animate a radial fill gradient.

Switches the fill mode when the pending delay expires, then interpolates the numeric/color fields. Replaces other solid/gradient fill-color tracks while preserving fill opacity. Resolves lineStyle_Auto as a fill.

\param center Target gradient center.
\param centerColor Target color at the center.
\param radius Finite target gradient radius in geometry units.
\param endColor Target color at the outer radius.
\param seconds Required finite, nonnegative duration in seconds. Zero applies the target immediately. After wait() or andThen(), duration must be greater than zero.
\param easing EasingFunc callback, such as linearTween or easeInOutQuad. Its elapsed time and duration arguments are seconds.

\return This object, for chaining.

\see changeFillGradient(), changeFillColor()
*/

/**
\fn AnimatedAttributesBase & pdg::AnimatedAttributesBase::changeSphereRotation(float radians, double seconds, EasingFunc easing, int direction)
\brief Animate spherical-image rotation.

Targets an absolute angle. Replaces the matching track and consumes the pending wait. Positive angles rotate clockwise.

\param radians Finite target angle in radians.
\param seconds Required finite, nonnegative duration in seconds. Zero applies the target immediately. After wait() or andThen(), duration must be greater than zero.
\param easing EasingFunc callback, such as linearTween or easeInOutQuad. Its elapsed time and duration arguments are seconds.
\param direction Integer pdg::rotationDirection_AsSpecified, Shortest, Clockwise, or CounterClockwise (each with the rotationDirection_ prefix). AsSpecified preserves the requested number of turns.

\return This object, for chaining.

\see sphereRotation(), AnimatedBase::wait()
*/

/**
\fn AnimatedAttributesBase & pdg::AnimatedAttributesBase::changeFrames(int first, int last, double seconds, EasingFunc easing)
\brief Animate an inclusive sequence of image frames.

Visits first through last in either direction, with equal time per frame before easing. Does not loop; holds the last frame at completion. A pending wait keeps the old frame until the sequence starts. Calling frame() directly cancels the sequence.

\param first First integer frame index.
\param last Last integer frame index, inclusive.
\param seconds Finite nonnegative duration of the entire sequence, in seconds. Zero selects the last frame when the delay expires.
\param easing EasingFunc callback, such as linearTween or easeInOutQuad. Its elapsed time and duration arguments are seconds.

\return This object, for chaining.

\see frame(), AnimatedBase::wait()
*/

/**
\fn AnimatedAttributesBase & pdg::AnimatedAttributesBase::changeSkew(float x, float y, double seconds, EasingFunc easing)
\brief Animate a relative shear.

Postmultiplies the current transform by the requested shear, then interpolates the six affine matrix coefficients. This can pass through singular matrices. Replaces transform tracks; a later component request interrupts the matrix tween.

\param x Finite coefficient added to y per unit x.
\param y Finite coefficient added to x per unit y.
\param seconds Required finite, nonnegative duration in seconds. Zero applies the target immediately. After wait() or andThen(), duration must be greater than zero.
\param easing EasingFunc callback, such as linearTween or easeInOutQuad. Its elapsed time and duration arguments are seconds.

\return This object, for chaining.

\see changeTransform(), skew()
*/

/**
\fn AnimatedAttributesBase & pdg::AnimatedAttributesBase::changeTransform(const glm::mat3 & target, double seconds, EasingFunc easing)
\brief Animate a replacement affine matrix.

Interpolates the six affine coefficients, which may pass through singular matrices. Preserves logical width and height; matrix scale updates the independent scale components. A nonzero matrix column requires a nonzero corresponding logical dimension. Use rotateTo() for angular paths and direction control. Replaces transform tracks and constant transform rates; subsequent component requests interrupt the matrix tween at its current sample.

\param target Finite affine glm::mat3 in column-major layout: columns (a,b,0), (c,d,0), (tx,ty,1).
\param seconds Required finite, nonnegative duration in seconds. Zero applies the target immediately. After wait() or andThen(), duration must be greater than zero.
\param easing EasingFunc callback, such as linearTween or easeInOutQuad. Its elapsed time and duration arguments are seconds.

\return This object, for chaining.

\see rotateTo(), changeSkew(), setTransform()
*/



/**
\fn const glm::mat3 & pdg::AnimatedAttributesBase::getTransform() const
\brief Read the currently sampled shared transform.

Combines location, pivot, rotation, shear, logical size, independent scale, and inverse pivot. The returned reference changes as this object animates.

\return A const reference to the current affine glm::mat3; copy it to retain a sample.

\see setTransform(), changeTransform()
*/

/**
\fn Attributes & pdg::Attributes::setTransform(const glm::mat3 & matrix)
\brief Replace the affine transform immediately.

Unlike transform(), replaces the matrix instead of postmultiplying it. AnimatedAttributes preserves its logical width and height, updates its shared Animated transform components, and cancels conflicting transform animation. It rejects nonfinite or non-affine matrices and nonzero matrix columns whose corresponding logical dimension is zero.

\param matrix Finite affine glm::mat3 in column-major layout: columns (a,b,0), (c,d,0), (tx,ty,1).

\return This object, for chaining.

\see transform(), getTransform()
*/

/**
\fn AnimatedBase& pdg::AnimatedBase::changeMovementTo(const Vector& movement, double durationSeconds, EasingFunc easing)
\brief Animate the movement rate to a target.

Uses distance units per second. Duration is the time to reach the new rate, not the lifetime of the motion: the final rate continues until changed or stopped. Dynamic PhysicsBody owners reject this operation. Durations are explicit floating-point seconds. Zero applies immediately; a request after wait() or andThen() must have positive duration.

\param movement Movement rate in distance units per second.
\param durationSeconds Finite nonnegative duration in seconds.
\param easing EasingFunc callback; elapsed time and duration use seconds.

\return This object, for chaining.

\see setMovement(), changeMovementBy()
*/

/**
\fn AnimatedBase& pdg::AnimatedBase::changeMovementTo(float xPerSecond, float yPerSecond, double durationSeconds, EasingFunc easing)
\brief Animate the movement rate to a target.

Uses distance units per second. Duration is the time to reach the new rate, not the lifetime of the motion: the final rate continues until changed or stopped. Dynamic PhysicsBody owners reject this operation. Durations are explicit floating-point seconds. Zero applies immediately; a request after wait() or andThen() must have positive duration.

\param xPerSecond Movement rate in distance units per second.
\param yPerSecond Movement rate in distance units per second.
\param durationSeconds Finite nonnegative duration in seconds.
\param easing EasingFunc callback; elapsed time and duration use seconds.

\return This object, for chaining.

\see setMovement(), changeMovementBy()
*/




/**
\fn Offset pdg::AnimatedBase::getMovement() const
\brief Read the programmed movement rate.

Returns x/y distance units per second, independently of physical velocity.


\return The current value, returned as a new coordinate object.

\see setMovement(), changeMovementTo(), stopMovement()
*/


/**
\fn AnimatedBase& pdg::AnimatedBase::changeGrowingTo(float amountPerSecond, double durationSeconds, EasingFunc easing)
\brief Animate the growing rate to a target.

Uses logical units per second equally on both axes. Duration is the time to reach the new rate, not the lifetime of the motion: the final rate continues until changed or stopped. Logical size changes independently of transform scale. Durations are explicit floating-point seconds. Zero applies immediately; a request after wait() or andThen() must have positive duration.

\param amountPerSecond Growth rate in logical size units per second.
\param durationSeconds Finite nonnegative duration in seconds.
\param easing EasingFunc callback; elapsed time and duration use seconds.

\return This object, for chaining.

\see setGrowing(), changeGrowingBy()
*/


/**
\fn AnimatedBase& pdg::AnimatedBase::changeStretchingTo(float widthPerSecond, float heightPerSecond, double durationSeconds, EasingFunc easing)
\brief Animate the stretching rate to a target.

Uses logical units per second independently on each axis. Duration is the time to reach the new rate, not the lifetime of the motion: the final rate continues until changed or stopped. Logical size changes independently of transform scale. Durations are explicit floating-point seconds. Zero applies immediately; a request after wait() or andThen() must have positive duration.

\param widthPerSecond Growth rate in logical size units per second.
\param heightPerSecond Growth rate in logical size units per second.
\param durationSeconds Finite nonnegative duration in seconds.
\param easing EasingFunc callback; elapsed time and duration use seconds.

\return This object, for chaining.

\see setStretching(), changeStretchingBy()
*/


/**
\fn Offset pdg::AnimatedBase::getStretching() const
\brief Read logical width and height change rates.

Returns additive logical units per second for each dimension.


\return The current value, returned as a new coordinate object.

\see setStretching(), changeStretchingTo(), stopStretching()
*/


/**
\fn AnimatedBase& pdg::AnimatedBase::setScale(float x, float y)
\brief Set independent transform scale immediately.

Cancels scheduled scale changes. Logical width and height are unchanged. Negative scale reflects an axis; zero collapses it and makes the transform singular. The single-value overload sets both axes. This is an immediate, chainable operation. It throws after wait() or andThen(); only operations with a duration can be part of a timed animation sequence.

\param x Dimensionless scale factor.
\param y Dimensionless scale factor.

\return This object, for chaining.

\see getScale(), changeScaleTo(), changeScaleBy()
*/

/**
\fn AnimatedBase& pdg::AnimatedBase::setScale(float scale)
\brief Set independent transform scale immediately.

Cancels scheduled scale changes. Logical width and height are unchanged. Negative scale reflects an axis; zero collapses it and makes the transform singular. The single-value overload sets both axes. This is an immediate, chainable operation. It throws after wait() or andThen(); only operations with a duration can be part of a timed animation sequence.

\param scale Dimensionless scale factor.

\return This object, for chaining.

\see getScale(), changeScaleTo(), changeScaleBy()
*/




/**
\fn Offset pdg::AnimatedBase::getScale() const
\brief Read independent transform scale.

Returns dimensionless x/y scale factors; the initial factors are (1,1).


\return The current value, returned as a new coordinate object.

\see setScale(), changeScaleTo(), changeScaleBy()
*/


/**
\fn AnimatedBase& pdg::AnimatedBase::changeScaleTo(float x, float y, double durationSeconds, EasingFunc easing)
\brief Animate independent scale to a target.

Does not change logical size. Negative scale reflects; zero collapses an axis and makes inverse transforms singular. Durations are explicit floating-point seconds. Zero applies immediately; a request after wait() or andThen() must have positive duration.

\param x Dimensionless scale factor.
\param y Dimensionless scale factor.
\param durationSeconds Finite nonnegative duration in seconds.
\param easing EasingFunc callback; elapsed time and duration use seconds.

\return This object, for chaining.

\see setScale(), getScale(), changeScaleBy()
*/


/**
\fn AnimatedBase& pdg::AnimatedBase::pauseSchedule()
\brief Pause scheduled animations and their delays.

Constant rates, helpers, clips and physics continue. Pausing a rate transition holds its current rate while movement, spin or growth continues at that rate.


\return This object, for chaining.

\see resumeSchedule(), isSchedulePaused(), cancelSchedule()
*/


/**
\fn AnimatedBase& pdg::AnimatedBase::resumeSchedule()
\brief Resume scheduled animations and their delays.

Resumes from the current samples without advancing through the paused time.


\return This object, for chaining.

\see pauseSchedule(), isSchedulePaused()
*/


/**
\fn AnimatedBase& pdg::AnimatedBase::cancelSchedule()
\brief Discard scheduled animations and pending sequencing.

Keeps current values, constant rates and the paused state. Clears pending wait()/andThen() state. AnimatedAttributes also clears appearance and frame transitions.


\return This object, for chaining.

\see hasScheduledAnimations(), pauseSchedule()
*/


/**
\fn bool pdg::AnimatedBase::isSchedulePaused() const
\brief Test whether scheduled animation advancement is paused.

This does not report whether constant motion or physics is running.


\return True if scheduled animation is paused.

\see pauseSchedule(), resumeSchedule()
*/


/**
\fn bool pdg::AnimatedBase::hasScheduledAnimations() const
\brief Test whether animations are running or queued.

A pending wait() or andThen() alone does not count as a scheduled animation.


\return True if an animation is running or queued.

\see cancelSchedule(), andThen()
*/


/**
\fn AnimatedBase& pdg::AnimatedBase::rotateTo(float radiansRotation)
\brief Rotate to an absolute angle.

The immediate overload preserves spin. The timed overload replaces spin and follows the selected angular route. This is an immediate, chainable operation. It throws after wait() or andThen(); only operations with a duration can be part of a timed animation sequence.

\param radiansRotation Angle in radians, clockwise positive.

\return This object, for chaining.

\see setRotation(), rotateBy(), changeSpinTo()
*/

/**
\fn AnimatedBase& pdg::AnimatedBase::rotateTo(float radiansRotation, double durationSeconds, EasingFunc easing, int direction)
\brief Rotate to an absolute angle.

The immediate overload preserves spin. The timed overload replaces spin and follows the selected angular route. Durations are explicit floating-point seconds. Zero applies immediately; a request after wait() or andThen() must have positive duration.

\param radiansRotation Angle in radians, clockwise positive.
\param durationSeconds Finite nonnegative duration in seconds.
\param easing EasingFunc callback; elapsed time and duration use seconds.
\param direction Integer rotationDirection_AsSpecified, rotationDirection_Shortest, rotationDirection_Clockwise or rotationDirection_CounterClockwise constant.

\return This object, for chaining.

\see setRotation(), rotateBy(), changeSpinTo()
*/


/**
\fn AnimatedBase& pdg::AnimatedBase::rotateBy(float radians)
\brief Rotate through a relative angle.

The immediate overload preserves spin. The timed overload replaces spin and retains explicitly requested complete turns. Clockwise/counterclockwise direction selects the sign; AsSpecified and Shortest preserve the signed relative travel. This is an immediate, chainable operation. It throws after wait() or andThen(); only operations with a duration can be part of a timed animation sequence.

\param radians Angle in radians, clockwise positive.

\return This object, for chaining.

\see rotateTo(), setRotation(), changeSpinBy()
*/

/**
\fn AnimatedBase& pdg::AnimatedBase::rotateBy(float radians, double durationSeconds, EasingFunc easing, int direction)
\brief Rotate through a relative angle.

The immediate overload preserves spin. The timed overload replaces spin and retains explicitly requested complete turns. Clockwise/counterclockwise direction selects the sign; AsSpecified and Shortest preserve the signed relative travel. Durations are explicit floating-point seconds. Zero applies immediately; a request after wait() or andThen() must have positive duration.

\param radians Angle in radians, clockwise positive.
\param durationSeconds Finite nonnegative duration in seconds.
\param easing EasingFunc callback; elapsed time and duration use seconds.
\param direction Integer rotationDirection_AsSpecified, rotationDirection_Shortest, rotationDirection_Clockwise or rotationDirection_CounterClockwise constant.

\return This object, for chaining.

\see rotateTo(), setRotation(), changeSpinBy()
*/


/**
\fn AnimatedBase& pdg::AnimatedBase::changeSpinTo(float radiansPerSecond, double durationSeconds, EasingFunc easing)
\brief Animate the spin rate to a target.

Uses clockwise radians per second. Duration is the time to reach the new rate, not the lifetime of the motion: the final rate continues until changed or stopped. Dynamic PhysicsBody owners reject this operation. Durations are explicit floating-point seconds. Zero applies immediately; a request after wait() or andThen() must have positive duration.

\param radiansPerSecond Angular rate in clockwise radians per second.
\param durationSeconds Finite nonnegative duration in seconds.
\param easing EasingFunc callback; elapsed time and duration use seconds.

\return This object, for chaining.

\see setSpin(), changeSpinBy()
*/






/**
\fn AnimatedBase& pdg::AnimatedBase::wait(double durationSeconds)
\brief Delay the next timed animation request.

The next timed operation consumes this delay. Replaces pending sequencing. Immediate setters and operations cannot follow it until a timed request consumes it or cancelSchedule() clears it, even for wait(0).

\param durationSeconds Finite nonnegative delay in seconds.

\return This object, for chaining.

\see andThen(), cancelSchedule()
*/


/**
\fn AnimatedBase & pdg::AnimatedBase::addAnimationHelper(IAnimationHelper* helper)
\brief Register a callback after the object's normal animation step.

Helpers run in registration order and receive elapsed floating-point seconds.
Returning false retires that registration. Adding an already registered helper is
an idempotent operation. A helper may be registered on several owners.

Removing or clearing helpers inside a callback skips their remaining calls on this
step. Newly added registrations run on the next step. Removing and re-adding the
current helper creates a new registration; returning false only retires the old one.

\param helper Non-null callback object. If ownedByAnimated() returns true,
each owner retains a native reference until registration ends and any executing
callback returns. The final release deletes the helper. Keep your own addRef()/release()
reference if you intend to reuse a helper after retirement. A borrowed helper
(ownedByAnimated() returns false) must outlive its registrations and executing callbacks.
\return This object, for chaining.
\see removeAnimationHelper(), clearAnimationHelpers(), IAnimationHelper
*/

/**
\fn AnimatedBase & pdg::AnimatedBase::removeAnimationHelper(IAnimationHelper* helper)
\brief Retire this owner's registration of a helper.

An absent helper is ignored. Owned helpers release their registration reference
once any in-flight callback has returned; other owners' registrations are unaffected.
\param helper Helper to remove from this object.
\return This object, for chaining.
\see addAnimationHelper(), clearAnimationHelpers()
*/

/**
\fn AnimatedBase & pdg::AnimatedBase::clearAnimationHelpers()
\brief Retire all helpers registered on this object.

Safe inside a helper callback. Remaining callbacks on this step are skipped, and
owned helpers are released after executing callbacks return.
\return This object, for chaining.
\see addAnimationHelper(), removeAnimationHelper()
*/

/**
\fn AnimatedBase& pdg::AnimatedBase::andThen()
\brief Start the next timed operation after the most recently scheduled operation finishes.

Uses its remaining duration and delay, not the longest of all concurrent operations. The next request consumes this sequencing state; later calls again run concurrently unless sequenced. A successor acquires its properties and resolves relative targets when it starts. Immediate calls after andThen() throw even if the predecessor has finished. No then() method is provided, so returning an Animated object from an async JavaScript function is safe.


\return This object, for chaining.

\see wait(), cancelSchedule()
*/
